import sys; sys.path.insert(0,'..')
from con_helpers import *
save(4,5,"The Inherited Mind: Grooves and the Move That Is Yours","Catch one groove in the act and name it, 'this is the loop, not the news'. Then plant one small, truer groove beside it by repetition, not by force.",[
mc("U","What is a saṁskāra, as Conversation 4.5 describes it?",
 ["A groove worn into the mind that sustains itself and fires on its own when a matching cue arrives.","A thought held in mind on purpose, which fades from view as soon as the person stops attending.","A rule of conduct taught to a child, which stays in force for so long as the child obeys it.","A rare kind of memory belonging to a few gifted people, which most others do not have at all."],0,
 "The first time something happens it leaves a faint trace; repeated or intense enough, the trace becomes a self-feeding loop like the doughnut. It sustains itself, is woken by a cue and not created by it, deepens each time it runs, and does not cancel the others.",
 "A saṁskāra is a self-sustaining groove","Part 1"),
mc("A","Sarah’s morning dread comes before she has done anything. What does Swamiji say it is?",
 ["A groove spinning up again: the loop, not news from her life, so she names it, not argues.","A true warning about something wrong in her life, which she should look into at once.","A sign of a weak character, which will go once she has made up her mind to be stronger.","A passing thing with no pattern, which comes from nowhere and is best left quite alone."],0,
 "It comes before she makes it, with the same weight and timing, as if it had its own autobiography. It was woken by a cue, not made by it, which is why it fires so fast: it was already there.",
 "The morning dread is a groove, not news","Part 1"),
judge("“Saṁskāras are the enemy, and the aim of practice is to get rid of every one of them.”",F,
 "A pianist playing without thinking about her fingers, a surgeon’s steady hands and a kind person’s instant move toward someone in distress are all grooves. Grooves are what we are at the level of everyday mind. The question is which serve fit and which feed the drift.",
 "Grooves are not the enemy; skill is a groove","Part 1"),
mc("A","Kai asks why old patterns get harder to break the longer they have run. What does Swamiji say?",
 ["Not because he is weak: the groove gains energy each time it runs, by the same repetition.","Because he has not tried hard enough, so the groove is still as strong as it was at the start.","Because patterns are fixed at birth, and the years that follow can do nothing to change them.","Because the longer a pattern runs the more it is loved, and so the less he wishes to let it go."],0,
 "The very thing that lets a groove form, repetition wearing a loop, lets a new groove form. You cannot delete the old loop by force, but you can starve it a little each time you do not follow it, and wear a new, truer one beside it.",
 "Repetition deepens a groove, and can make a new one","Part 1"),
mc("U","How does Swamiji distinguish a saṁskāra from a vāsanā?",
 ["A saṁskāra fires in a moment; a vāsanā is a larger disposition that colours a whole life.","A saṁskāra is good and a vāsanā is bad, so that the two should be treated quite differently.","A saṁskāra belongs to the body and a vāsanā to the mind, so that they never act together.","A saṁskāra is learned and a vāsanā is inborn, so that a person can do nothing to alter it."],0,
 "Grooves that share a theme couple into a standing orientation. The epic’s Duryodhana says he knows what is right and cannot bring himself to do it: a lifetime of separate grooves coupled into one disposition of enmity, the very lens he saw through.",
 "A vāsanā couples grooves into a disposition","Part 1"),
judge("“The laboratory account and the contemplative account agree on where grooves finally reside and whether they outlast the body.”",F,
 "They substantially agree on what grooves are and do: self-sustaining, cue-fired, reinforced by repetition. They diverge on where they finally reside and whether they survive death. Swamiji asks us to hold that open, and notes the practice is identical either way.",
 "The accounts agree on function, not on residence","Part 2"),
mc("A","Dr Watson wants to offer a patient agency without asking him to fix his whole past. What does Swamiji suggest?",
 ["Locate it in the next response: the reservoir and the hand dealt are not chosen, but the move that is his is.","Tell him that his past does not matter, so that he can begin again as if none of it had happened.","Tell him to find the cause of each groove in turn, since a groove can be put right only when it is traced.","Tell him it is all fate and nothing he does can matter, so that he is spared the burden of effort."],0,
 "The tradition splits the inheritance by time: the whole accumulated store, the part active in this life, and what you are forming now. Freedom is not in choosing your hand but in playing it, and every conscious response writes what ripens next.",
 "Agency lives in the next response","Part 3"),
mc("A","Kai asks whether this is just working harder than your conditioning. How does Swamiji answer?",
 ["Practice builds grooves that lift the system to a higher pitch, so a deep cut can land as a pin-prick.","Yes, it is a contest of strength with the past, and the stronger will wins, so it needs a great deal of effort.","No effort is needed, since the past will undo itself in time if a person simply waits and does nothing.","It is just positive thinking, which means saying good things to oneself until the old patterns give way."],0,
 "A strong tuning fork sets a weaker one humming: you provide the direction and the higher source the lift your effort alone could not. Practice does not stop life happening; it changes how hard it lands. This is the structural action of practice, not a metaphor for comfort.",
 "Practice lifts the field a blow passes through","Part 3"),
mc("A","Sarah is asked to name her morning groove and plant a new one. What does she do?",
 ["Name it as the loop, not the news, without arguing, then do one small kind thing at the same time each day.","Argue with the dread until it gives way, then rest for the remainder of the day to recover.","Ignore the dread and fill the morning with tasks, so that no space is left in which it could arise.","Wait for the dread to pass, then plan a large change in her life to be made in the new year."],0,
 "A slow breath at the window, or one true sentence to herself, same time every day. Repetition is the whole mechanism: she is wearing a new groove beside the old, not erasing it.",
 "Name the loop, then plant a small daily kindness","Practical situation"),
mc("A","Kai tests his returning loop. What exactly does he try, and what is he measuring?",
 ["Neither feeding nor fighting it, and whether it returns a little less fiercely over the week.","Pushing it away each time it comes, and whether it goes for longer in the course of the week.","Following it through to the end each time, and whether it has any more to say after that.","Writing down every time it comes, and whether the number of times falls by the end of the week."],0,
 "He tests whether starving a groove rather than fighting it shallows it. It is a clean experiment, in the spirit of finding it out for oneself.",
 "Starve the groove: do not feed it, do not fight it","Practical situation")])
